(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "adr-2609010300-a-discovery-surface-is-a-promise-and-nothing-was-checking-it")
  (:adr/title "A discovery surface is a promise, and nothing was checking it")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-09-01")
  (:adr/related (vector "adr-2608319800-the-itonami-economy-sells-answers-and-the-constraint-is-demand"
                "adr-2609010200-a-faucet-gated-by-a-passkey-issues-the-grant-not-only-the-tokens"))
  (:adr/body "x402.nexus now answers the four documents an agent looks for, and a
detector checks that what they name is actually served.

## What shipped

- `GET /.well-known/mcp.json` -- MCP manifest, three tools.
- `POST /mcp` -- JSON-RPC. `tools/list`, `tools/call`.
- `GET /openapi.json` -- OpenAPI 3.1 for clients that read OpenAPI.
- `GET /skill.md` -- SKILL.md for an agent that needs to buy here.
- `/llms.txt` and the index page now name all of the above.
- Unrouted paths answer 404. They used to answer the index page with 200.

The tools are `list_catalog`, `service_stats` and `get_payment_challenge`.
Each runs the same internal function its HTTP route runs. `apply_as_seller`
was withdrawn: its only execution path was a self-fetch, and a POST with a
side effect is not something to make work by adding a second way to invoke
it. `/apply` stays in the OpenAPI document.

## The defect class this surfaced, three times in one session

Each of these looked like success from outside.

1. Four endpoints were reported as already live because each answered 200.
   The worker served its index page for every unrouted path, so a nonsense
   path returned the same bytes. Only one of the four existed. The fix to
   the report was one request to a path nobody could have advertised.

2. `/.well-known/mcp.json` served a well-formed manifest advertising zero
   tools. The renderer was called in the JSON-to-EDN direction, so it looked
   for string keys in an EDN map, found none, and returned an empty model
   rather than an error. The wrong direction returned a plausible empty.

3. A tool in that manifest reached its endpoint by fetching the worker's own
   origin. Cloudflare answered 522 -- a Worker calling its own hostname is a
   subrequest back through the edge -- and the tool returned that error text
   as its result, wrapped in a 200.

A fourth, adjacent: the release build compiled a call to a `gateway`
function that does not exist in that namespace. shadow-cljs reported it as
`:undeclared-var`, which is the same warning it reports for a benign forward
reference, and three benign ones sat beside it in a build carrying thirteen
warnings. The thrown error surfaced to the caller as `parse error`, naming
the request rather than the code. The forms were moved below every handler
they call, so the file now has zero undeclared-var warnings and the next one
will stand alone.

## The detector, and why the negative control is the whole of it

`scripts/verify-advertised-surfaces.cljs`, registered as
`:verify-advertised-surfaces`. It reads a host's own `/llms.txt` and
`/openapi.json`, extracts the same-host endpoints they name, and probes each
one.

Checking that an advertised path answers is not enough, and defect 1 is why:
every path answered. What separates a served path from an unserved one is
that its response differs from what the host says to a path nobody could
have advertised. So the control is fetched first, and a body-for-body match
is a finding however healthy its status code looks.

A 404 alone is not a finding. A host that says no is telling the truth; it
counts only where a document promised that path.

Every red the detector produced before it had controls was its own
extraction bug: GET-probing endpoints the document named POST, a placeholder
truncated to its prefix (`/gateway/<seller>/<path>` becoming `/gateway/`),
and fragment links that fetch the same document. All three are now
self-test cases. The discriminating run is against a local host serving the
two real shapes: a path advertised and answering 404 reports `absent`, the
same path answering the index reports `same-as-control`, a path with a
distinct body is clean, and a POST-only path advertised as POST answers 402
and is clean -- which is what proves the method is read from the document
rather than assumed.

Measured 2026-09-01: 9 advertised paths on x402.nexus and 40 on
itonami.cloud, both clean after the fixes.

## Running it across the estate

Ten of the 425 non-wildcard hosts in the surface index answer /llms.txt.
Folding www and alias names leaves five documents, plus itonami.cloud which
is not in that index. 81 advertised paths across those six.

Three standing findings, each confirmed by hand:

- atproto.etzhayyim.com/.well-known/did.json and
  /.well-known/oauth-authorization-server answer Cloudflare 1033 -- a
  hostname proxied by Cloudflare with nothing behind it. etzhayyim.com's own
  llms.txt promises that host, and no repo in the surface index claims it.
- mcp.etzhayyim.com/xrpc/com.etzhayyim.mcp.message does not resolve in DNS at
  all, although orgs/kotoba-lang/app-agentgateway declares that host.

And one host reported UNMEASURED rather than red. yoro.etzhayyim.com
redirects every path to aozora.app, which returns the same 2455 bytes for
/search and for a path nobody routed. That is what a single-page app does,
and this workspace requires single-page apps, so a served path and an
unserved one genuinely cannot be told apart there from outside. Calling it a
defect would be a standing red nobody should clear; calling it clean would
be the lie this detector exists to prevent. The UNMEASURED lines print with
or without --findings.

## Six of its own bugs, in the order they were found

Every red this detector produced before it had controls was its own:

1. GET-probing endpoints a document named POST.
2. A placeholder truncated to its prefix: `/gateway/<seller>/<path>` became
   `/gateway/`.
3. Fragment links, which fetch the same document by definition.
4. Globs -- `/api/*` is a shape, not an address.
5. Reading an HTML index served at /openapi.json as if it were a discovery
   document, then reporting that index's own links as broken promises. It
   committed the failure mode it was written to detect.
6. A registrable-domain helper threaded with ->> instead of ->, which put the
   string into str/split's regex position so every host returned the same
   value, every host compared equal, and the same-site filter accepted
   everything including foreign domains. Two self-test cases asserting that a
   foreign domain is dropped are what caught it; without them this would have
   shipped as a widening that looks exactly like a working filter.

A seventh was caught by the harness rather than by a test: after the
same-site change, the local discriminating host (127.0.0.1:8791) compared
`0.1:8791` against `0.1`, every endpoint was dropped as off-site, and the one
run that makes this detector go red silently stopped making it go red. The
detector still exited 2, so it did not report clean -- but the reason had
changed and nothing said so.

All seven are self-test cases. 24 of them.

## What this does not change

The binding constraint is still demand, not surface. The catalog holds 25
priced resources across 7 sellers and external payments remain at one, for
$0.001, on 2026-08-14. Making the shop easier to find does not make anyone
buy. This work was worth doing because an agent that cannot discover the
terms cannot pay them either, but it is not evidence of demand and should
not be read as any.

The faucet, our own Base Sepolia ERC-20, and funding the dougaka buyer at
0x38C2229589e2097486b4a3390f557E84cB69F30c all remain blocked on one
external gas drip. Base is an OP-Stack rollup with difficulty 0 and a fixed
miner address, so no fleet can produce testnet gas by mining; only a token
can be issued, and issuing one still costs gas.")))
